WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.941
Digital signature systems are built around public and private key pairs.

2
00:00:03.941 --> 00:00:07.056
The private key creates signatures and must be protected.

3
00:00:07.056 --> 00:00:10.616
The public key verifies signatures and may be widely distributed.

4
00:00:10.616 --> 00:00:20.724
The security of the system depends on keeping the private key under the
control of the intended signer and giving verifiers a trustworthy way to
know which public key belongs to which signer.

5
00:00:20.724 --> 00:00:24.856
Public key distribution is a trust problem, not just a file transfer
problem.

6
00:00:24.856 --> 00:00:30.005
A verifier needs confidence that the public key it uses actually belongs
to the expected signer.

7
00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:38.969
Trust anchors, key identifiers, certificates at a high level, and
certificate chains at a high level are mechanisms for answering that
question in different systems.

8
00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:47.170
A self-managed key can be appropriate in a closed environment, while
externally issued certificates may be appropriate when broader trust is
required.

9
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:52.573
Key identifiers help applications select the right verification key,
especially during rotation.

10
00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:54.671
They do not create trust by themselves.

11
00:00:54.671 --> 00:01:01.727
An attacker-controlled key with a familiar-looking identifier is not
trustworthy just because the identifier matches a pattern.

12
00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:08.212
Verifiers need rules for which keys are accepted, where those keys come
from, who owns them, and how changes are reviewed.

13
00:01:08.212 --> 00:01:14.887
A valid signature generally proves possession of the corresponding private
key for the signed data under the chosen scheme.

14
00:01:14.887 --> 00:01:22.769
It does not prove the signer is allowed to perform a business action, that
the signed content is safe, or that the message should be accepted
forever.

15
00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:28.745
Trust decisions must connect key control, expected signer, intended use,
freshness, and application policy.

16
00:01:28.745 --> 00:01:33.640
Otherwise any valid signature from any key can become a dangerously broad
acceptance rule.
